div.p:
17. (διὰ τὰς
) ... ἐφόδους
—their hazardous intercourse.

13. τοῦ ... πλοῦ
=περὶ τοῦ πλοῦ, anticipating ὅπῃ κομισθήσονται: the gen. placed thus at the head of a sentence with a verb
of speaking or thinking about is common in Plato; and the constn. comes down from epic. For trag. cf. Soph. OT 317, Trach. 169 τοῦ κασιγνήτου τί φῄς ; Plat. Rep. 576 D εὐδαιμονίας ὡσαύτως ἢ ἄλλως πως κρίνεις ;

18. (ὑπὸ
) ἀνέμου, ὅς
—a wind which.

20. [ἔπρασσον

									]—this
								spoils the structure of the period, for the principal sentence must
								begin at τότε δή. There is, for
								example, a similar intrusion of a verb—μετεῖχον—in 2.16; 
										ἔξεστιν in viii. 27.

24. καὶ ὅσοι κτλ

									.—see c. 89. 2;
								those who had revolted from Persia after Mycale.

23. οἱ ἐξιόντες

									—cp. c. 77. 6;
								the pres. of the succession of commanders.

12 εἰδότες

									 —after τριήρεις, of the crews, as often;
								e.g. vi. 104. 1.

16 προαγορεύειν

									—depending on εἶπον, but in a different relation;
								cf. c. 26. 5.

19. τῶν
[ τε
]—the τε has no correlative and is no doubt spurious.

16. σφίσιν ἐπιτηδείως

									—see c. 19;
								alluding to the form of constitution. The Schol. says he thinks τοῖς Λ. is a gloss on σφίσι.

4. ἐς τάδε προήγαγον αὐτά

									 —brought our empire to this;
								repeated almost in the same words by Alcibiades in 6.18.
